WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:04.080
The extra frame volume can fill quickly when equipment, network connections, power

2
00:00:04.160 --> 00:00:06.000
paths and cooling lines are integrated.

3
00:00:06.520 --> 00:00:10.100
Routing is a shared spatial problem, not a final cosmetic step.

4
00:00:10.520 --> 00:00:13.620
Reserve the required envelopes before filling the remaining space.

5
00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:18.440
Check bend, support and strain requirements using the selected components and supplier

6
00:00:18.520 --> 00:00:18.920
guidance.

7
00:00:19.440 --> 00:00:24.100
Then review service movement: which item moves, which connections remain, and whether

8
00:00:24.140 --> 00:00:26.660
adjacent equipment can stay within its permitted state.

9
00:00:27.120 --> 00:00:30.260
A route that works at rest can become unacceptable during removal.

10
00:00:30.780 --> 00:00:33.480
Include containment and residual airflow where required.

11
00:00:33.920 --> 00:00:37.620
A hose or cable rearrangement can affect both service access and cooling.

12
00:00:38.100 --> 00:00:41.600
Record the accepted arrangement and who approves changes after deployment.

13
00:00:42.040 --> 00:00:45.380
This prevents a later addition from quietly occupying the clearance that the

14
00:00:45.420 --> 00:00:47.240
original integration review relied on.

15
00:00:47.700 --> 00:00:51.360
Wider packaging can offer useful options, but it doesn't make all internal

16
00:00:51.460 --> 00:00:54.260
space available for unrelated functions at the same time.

